        "original_id": "c00b5dd3-d029-5395-a9b4-052046f969ba",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Camassia, Chelone, camas root\n- Places: Camas County, Idaho, Fairfield, Idaho, Canada, United States, eastern North America\n- People: Native Americans, settlers\n\nFacts:\n- Camassia is a genus of plants in the asparagus family that is native to Canada and the United States.\n- Chelone is a genus of four species of perennial herbaceous plants that is native to eastern North America.\n- Camas County, Idaho, was named for the camas root, or Camassia, a lily-like plant with an edible bulb found in the region.\n- Native Americans and settlers used the camas root in the Camas County region as a food source.\n- Fairfield is the county seat of Camas County, Idaho, and the only city in the county.",
